The Security Council,

Recalling its resolutions 2042 (2012), 2043 (2012), 2139 (2014), 2165 (2014), 2175 (2014), 2191 (2014), 2258 (2015), 2332 (2016), 2393 (2017), 2401 (2018), 2449 (2018), 2504 (2020), 2533 (2020), 2585 (2021), 2642 (2022) and 2672 (2023), and the statements of its President,

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the Syrian Arab Republic, and to the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,

Determining that the devastating humanitarian situation in the Syrian Arab Republic continues to constitute a threat to peace and security in the region,

Underscoring that humanitarian assistance must be delivered in full compliance with the United Nations guiding principles of humanitarian emergency assistance as set out in General Assembly resolution 46/182, including the principles of humanity, neutrality, impartiality and independence, and with full respect for the sovereignty and territorial integrity of the Syrian Arab Republic,

Expressing grave concern over the continuing adverse impact of unilateral coercive measures on the humanitarian situation and on the capacity to respond to the needs of the Syrian people, including early recovery and post-earthquake reconstruction,

Stressing that the cross-border mechanism is an exceptional and temporary arrangement that must not undermine the sovereignty of the Syrian Arab Republic, and underlining the urgent necessity to progressively replace it with regular cross-line deliveries from within Syrian territory to all parts of the country without discrimination,

Welcoming all efforts to expand humanitarian activities across the Syrian Arab Republic, including through early recovery projects aimed at restoring basic infrastructure,

1. Decides to renew the decisions in paragraphs 2 and 3 of Security Council resolution 2165 (2014), for a period of six months, that is, until 10 January 2024, only for the border crossing Bab al-Hawa, and requests the Secretary-General to provide a special report on the humanitarian needs in the Syrian Arab Republic no later than 10 December 2023;

2. Requests the Secretary-General to present a comprehensive special report to the Security Council no later than 10 December 2023 on the humanitarian impact of unilateral coercive measures imposed on the Syrian Arab Republic;

3. Calls upon all Member States to respond with practical steps to address the urgent needs of the Syrian people, and encourages continued and expanded humanitarian assistance, including through funding for early recovery projects in water, sanitation, health, education, electricity, and shelter, as well as humanitarian mine action;

4. Demands the removal of all impediments to the delivery of humanitarian aid through cross-line modalities across all parts of the Syrian Arab Republic, and calls upon all parties to provide safe, unimpeded and sustained access for cross-line humanitarian operations;

5. Requests the Secretary-General to brief the Council monthly and to provide a report on a regular basis, at least every 60 days, on the implementation of resolutions 2139 (2014), 2165 (2014), 2191 (2014), 2258 (2015), 2332 (2016), 2393 (2017), 2401 (2018), 2449 (2018), 2504 (2020), 2533 (2020), 2585 (2021), 2642 (2022), 2672 (2023) and the present resolution;

6. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

